1
00:00:01,042 --> 00:00:04,087
Wo de zing
shi Sheldon.

2
00:00:04,129 --> 00:00:07,675
No, it's "Wo de ming
zi shi Sheldon."

3
00:00:07,716 --> 00:00:12,095
Wo de ming
zi shi Sheldon.

4
00:00:12,137 --> 00:00:15,098
What's this?

5
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:16,350
That's what you did.

6
00:00:16,391 --> 00:00:19,060
I assumed as in a
number of languages,

7
00:00:19,102 --> 00:00:20,771
that the gesture was
part of the phrase.

8
00:00:20,813 --> 00:00:23,022
Well, it's not.

9
00:00:23,064 --> 00:00:25,108
How am I supposed
to know that?

10
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:26,861
As the teacher, it's your
obligation to separate

11
00:00:26,902 --> 00:00:29,780
your personal
idiosyncrasies from
the subject matter.

12
00:00:29,822 --> 00:00:32,783
You know, I'm really glad
you decided to learn Mandarin.

13
00:00:32,825 --> 00:00:34,201
Why?
Once you're fluent,

14
00:00:34,242 --> 00:00:37,078
you'll have a billion more
people to annoy instead of me.

15
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:38,914
Hey.

16
00:00:38,956 --> 00:00:41,416
Mei du lui zi.

17
00:00:41,458 --> 00:00:46,171
You just called Leonard
a syphilitic donkey.

18
00:00:46,213 --> 00:00:48,047
My apologies,
Leonard.

19
00:00:48,089 --> 00:00:50,300
I'm only as good
as my teacher.

20
00:00:50,342 --> 00:00:52,553
Why are you
learning Chinese?

21
00:00:52,594 --> 00:00:54,137
I believe the
Szechuan Palace

22
00:00:54,179 --> 00:00:57,056
has been passing off orange
chicken as tangerine chicken,

23
00:00:57,098 --> 00:00:59,393
and I intend to
confront them.

24
00:00:59,434 --> 00:01:01,353
If I were you,
I'd be more concerned

25
00:01:01,395 --> 00:01:03,438
about what they're passing off
as chicken.

26
00:01:07,026 --> 00:01:08,276
I need to use your window.

27
00:01:08,318 --> 00:01:10,612
Oh, hey. Yeah, No, sure.
Go ahead.

28
00:01:11,697 --> 00:01:14,867
Hey, jerk face,
you forgot your iPod!

29
00:01:14,909 --> 00:01:15,826
(clattering)

30
00:01:15,868 --> 00:01:17,745
What's going on?

31
00:01:17,786 --> 00:01:19,454
Oh, I'll tell you
what's going on.

32
00:01:19,496 --> 00:01:21,749
That stupid self-centered
bastard wrote about our sex life

33
00:01:21,790 --> 00:01:22,833
in his blog!

34
00:01:25,168 --> 00:01:28,171
Drop dead, you stupid
self-centered bastard!

35
00:01:29,882 --> 00:01:31,508
Thank you.

36
00:01:34,887 --> 00:01:36,471
Okay, where were we?

37
00:01:36,513 --> 00:01:39,266
Not now. I have a blog to find.

38
00:01:41,184 --> 00:01:44,103
♪ Our whole universe
was in a hot, dense state ♪

39
00:01:44,145 --> 00:01:48,149
♪ Then nearly 14 billion years
ago expansion started... Wait! ♪

40
00:01:48,191 --> 00:01:49,818
♪ The Earth began to cool ♪

41
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:52,362
♪ The autotrophs began to drool,
Neanderthals developed tools ♪

42
00:01:52,404 --> 00:01:54,489
♪ We built the Wall ♪
♪ We built the pyramids ♪

43
00:01:54,531 --> 00:01:57,242
♪ Math, Science, History,
unraveling the mystery ♪

44
00:01:57,284 --> 00:01:59,411
♪ That all started
with a big bang ♪

45
00:01:59,453 --> 00:02:01,162
♪ Bang! ♪

46
00:02:09,922 --> 00:02:11,548
Penny, are you okay?

47
00:02:11,590 --> 00:02:13,926
I'm fine, Leonard.
Just go away.

48
00:02:13,968 --> 00:02:15,886
Look, I understand

49
00:02:15,928 --> 00:02:17,930
that breaking up with someone
can be very painful...

50
00:02:17,972 --> 00:02:18,931
Go away!

51
00:02:18,973 --> 00:02:20,891
Okay. Feel better! Bye.

52
00:02:25,270 --> 00:02:28,273
She doesn't want to talk.

53
00:02:28,315 --> 00:02:30,358
Not surprising.

54
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:31,902
Penny's emotional responses

55
00:02:31,944 --> 00:02:33,946
originate in the primitive
portion of the brain

56
00:02:33,988 --> 00:02:35,155
known as the amygdale,

57
00:02:35,196 --> 00:02:36,490
while speech is centered

58
00:02:36,531 --> 00:02:38,158
in the much more recently
developed neo-cortex.

59
00:02:38,199 --> 00:02:39,743
The former can easily overpower
the latter,

60
00:02:39,785 --> 00:02:40,995
giving scientific credence

61
00:02:41,036 --> 00:02:43,580
to the notion of being
"rendered speechless."

62
00:02:49,128 --> 00:02:51,880
Or maybe she just doesn't want
to talk.

63
00:02:54,215 --> 00:02:56,384
Hey, look, I found an iPod.

64
00:02:59,596 --> 00:03:01,431
It's smashed beyond repair.

65
00:03:01,473 --> 00:03:03,391
What are you gonna do with it?

66
00:03:03,433 --> 00:03:07,395
What else? Sell it on eBay
as "slightly used."

67
00:03:07,437 --> 00:03:09,982
It was Penny's boyfriend's.
They broke up.

68
00:03:10,024 --> 00:03:11,566
Apparently, he posted
intimate details

69
00:03:11,608 --> 00:03:14,235
of their physical
relationship on his blog,

70
00:03:14,277 --> 00:03:17,364
which I cannot find
anywhere.

71
00:03:17,405 --> 00:03:18,824
You know what?

72
00:03:18,866 --> 00:03:20,784
I'm gonna go back and try
talking to her again.

73
00:03:20,826 --> 00:03:21,785
Good idea.

74
00:03:21,827 --> 00:03:24,121
Sit with her,
hold her, comfort her.

75
00:03:24,163 --> 00:03:28,667
And if the moment feels right,
see if you can cop a feel.

76
00:03:28,709 --> 00:03:31,294
I'm not going to
do that, Howard.

77
00:03:31,336 --> 00:03:32,629
SHELDON:
I'm not aware

78
00:03:32,671 --> 00:03:33,964
of any social convention

79
00:03:34,006 --> 00:03:35,382
that requires you
to intervene at all.

80
00:03:35,423 --> 00:03:37,801
What about "damsel in distress"?

81
00:03:37,843 --> 00:03:39,594
12th-century code
of chivalry?

82
00:03:39,636 --> 00:03:41,680
Not exactly current.

83
00:03:41,722 --> 00:03:44,432
You'd also have to be knighted
for that to apply.

84
00:03:44,474 --> 00:03:46,060
I don't care.

85
00:03:46,101 --> 00:03:47,728
She's upset.
I'm going over there.

86
00:03:47,769 --> 00:03:49,730
Remember to sit
on your hands a bit

87
00:03:49,771 --> 00:03:52,066
so they're warm.

88
00:03:52,107 --> 00:03:53,483
I'm her friend.

89
00:03:53,525 --> 00:03:55,736
I'm not going to take advantage
of her vulnerability.

90
00:03:55,777 --> 00:03:56,611
Wha...?

91
00:03:56,653 --> 00:03:57,612
So you're saying

92
00:03:57,654 --> 00:03:59,114
if in the depths
of despair

93
00:03:59,156 --> 00:04:01,408
she throws herself at you
and demands you take her

94
00:04:01,449 --> 00:04:04,119
right there, right now,
you'll just walk away?

95
00:04:04,161 --> 00:04:06,496
I said I'm her friend,
not her gay friend.

96
00:04:10,375 --> 00:04:12,460
(knock at door)

97
00:04:12,502 --> 00:04:13,921
Hey, listen,
I know you said that

98
00:04:13,962 --> 00:04:15,338
you didn't want
to talk...
I don't.

99
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:17,340
Sorry.
Wait.

100
00:04:17,382 --> 00:04:19,718
Wait? Did you say "wait"?

101
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:22,179
Tell me the truth.

102
00:04:22,221 --> 00:04:26,141
Am I just an idiot who picks
giant losers?

103
00:04:26,183 --> 00:04:28,435
No, no.
Okay.

104
00:04:28,476 --> 00:04:31,312
So I pick good guys,
but turn them into losers?

105
00:04:31,354 --> 00:04:33,148
Of course not.

106
00:04:33,190 --> 00:04:35,734
Well, it's got to be
one or the other. Which is it?

107
00:04:40,614 --> 00:04:44,034
I'm sorry.
What were the choices again?

108
00:04:44,076 --> 00:04:46,411
I really thought
Mike was different.

109
00:04:46,453 --> 00:04:47,662
I thought he was

110
00:04:47,704 --> 00:04:49,497
sensitive and smart.

111
00:04:49,539 --> 00:04:53,252
I mean, not you smart--
normal non-freaky smart.

112
00:04:53,293 --> 00:04:54,544
Yeah. No, sure.
You know,

113
00:04:54,586 --> 00:04:56,922
and then he just goes
and has to humiliate me

114
00:04:56,964 --> 00:04:58,506
by writing about me on his blog

115
00:04:58,548 --> 00:05:00,717
so the entire world
can read it.

116
00:05:00,759 --> 00:05:03,511
I tell you, it's not all
that easy to find.

117
00:05:03,553 --> 00:05:05,555
Yeah, really? Well, my friends
at work found it,

118
00:05:05,597 --> 00:05:06,932
my sister found it.

119
00:05:06,974 --> 00:05:08,767
Judging by my email,
a number of prisoners

120
00:05:08,809 --> 00:05:11,853
at the Michigan State
Penitentiary found it.

121
00:05:11,895 --> 00:05:13,981
What exactly did
this guy write?

122
00:05:14,022 --> 00:05:17,400
Not that I need to know
the details of your sex life.

123
00:05:17,442 --> 00:05:18,152
I just thought...

124
00:05:18,193 --> 00:05:20,279
Never mind.

125
00:05:20,320 --> 00:05:21,529
No, you know what?

126
00:05:21,571 --> 00:05:24,283
Might as well read it.
Everybody else has.

127
00:05:24,324 --> 00:05:25,617
Go ahead.

128
00:05:25,659 --> 00:05:26,660
Oh, God, I just

129
00:05:26,701 --> 00:05:29,037
feel so betrayed
and embarrassed!

130
00:05:29,079 --> 00:05:31,748
I just want to crawl
into a hole and die!

131
00:05:34,709 --> 00:05:38,337
Okay. Well, you know
this isn't that bad.

132
00:05:38,379 --> 00:05:40,299
It just paints
the picture

133
00:05:40,339 --> 00:05:42,425
of a very affectionate woman,
who's open

134
00:05:42,467 --> 00:05:47,514
to expressing her affection
in non-traditional locales.

135
00:05:47,555 --> 00:05:49,516
Oh, God!

136
00:05:49,557 --> 00:05:53,770
Elevators, parks,
movie theaters.

137
00:05:53,812 --> 00:05:56,315
Out of curiosity,

138
00:05:56,355 --> 00:05:59,151
is this "subway"
the transportation system

139
00:05:59,193 --> 00:06:01,486
or Subway the sandwich shop?

140
00:06:01,528 --> 00:06:03,488
Sandwich shop.

141
00:06:03,530 --> 00:06:07,784
Doesn't that violate
the health code?

142
00:06:07,826 --> 00:06:10,453
No, at the sub shop
we were only making out.

143
00:06:10,495 --> 00:06:12,164
Ah, okay.

144
00:06:12,206 --> 00:06:14,373
But my point is that

145
00:06:14,415 --> 00:06:17,085
you have absolutely no reason
to be embarrassed.

146
00:06:17,127 --> 00:06:18,461
Really?

147
00:06:18,503 --> 00:06:19,754
Do you think
I overreacted?

148
00:06:19,796 --> 00:06:21,548
Maybe a little.

149
00:06:21,589 --> 00:06:23,508
Because I do that.
I do overreact.

150
00:06:23,550 --> 00:06:25,426
Maybe I should call Mike
and apologize.

151
00:06:25,468 --> 00:06:28,847
No, no, no.
That, that would
be under-reacting.

152
00:06:28,889 --> 00:06:30,640
He did break

153
00:06:30,682 --> 00:06:32,934
the implied confidentiality
of the bedroom.

154
00:06:32,976 --> 00:06:37,022
And in your case, the elevator,
parks and fast food franchise.

155
00:06:37,064 --> 00:06:38,523
You're right.

156
00:06:38,565 --> 00:06:41,026
I should just say
"I am done with him."

157
00:06:41,068 --> 00:06:42,944
Yes, you should.
Go ahead, say it.

158
00:06:42,986 --> 00:06:46,281
But I never gave the man
a chance to explain.

159
00:06:46,323 --> 00:06:48,033
What is there
to explain?

160
00:06:48,075 --> 00:06:49,492
It's all right here.

161
00:06:49,534 --> 00:06:51,410
The betrayal!

162
00:06:51,452 --> 00:06:54,122
No, you were right
the first time.

163
00:06:54,164 --> 00:06:57,416
This is a man who loves me,
but in his own stupid way,

164
00:06:57,458 --> 00:07:00,628
which is trying to show people
how he feels.

165
00:07:00,670 --> 00:07:05,342
I'm pretty sure
I never said that.

166
00:07:05,384 --> 00:07:06,509
No, you did better
than that.

167
00:07:06,551 --> 00:07:07,969
You helped me
see it on my own.

168
00:07:08,011 --> 00:07:11,056
Oh, good for me.

169
00:07:11,098 --> 00:07:12,391
Where are you going?

170
00:07:12,431 --> 00:07:13,892
I'm going over to Mike's.

171
00:07:13,934 --> 00:07:15,352
Leonard, thank you so much.

172
00:07:15,394 --> 00:07:17,687
Oh, sure.

173
00:07:21,775 --> 00:07:24,861
Huh, maybe I am her gay friend.

174
00:07:32,286 --> 00:07:34,329
Howard, I'm going
to need another
Mandarin lesson.

175
00:07:34,371 --> 00:07:35,538
I obviously didn't make

176
00:07:35,580 --> 00:07:38,750
my point with those people.

177
00:07:38,792 --> 00:07:40,043
For God's sake, Sheldon,

178
00:07:40,085 --> 00:07:41,586
if you don't like
the tangerine chicken,

179
00:07:41,628 --> 00:07:43,171
don't order
the tangerine chicken.

180
00:07:43,213 --> 00:07:44,464
I like tangerine chicken,

181
00:07:44,505 --> 00:07:46,633
I'm just not getting
tangerine chicken.

182
00:07:46,674 --> 00:07:49,594
Can we please change
the subject?

183
00:07:49,636 --> 00:07:50,595
Sure.

184
00:07:50,637 --> 00:07:52,347
Tell us again how you screwed up

185
00:07:52,389 --> 00:07:54,557
and got Penny back together
with her old boyfriend.

186
00:07:56,351 --> 00:07:58,477
Just roll the dice.

187
00:08:01,606 --> 00:08:03,608
"Enslaved by warlocks.

188
00:08:03,650 --> 00:08:05,652
Stay here until you roll
two, four or six."

189
00:08:05,693 --> 00:08:07,070
She was mad at him!

190
00:08:07,112 --> 00:08:09,156
She was done with him!

191
00:08:09,197 --> 00:08:11,199
The relationship was broken
beyond repair,

192
00:08:11,241 --> 00:08:14,577
and I walked over there,
and I fixed it.

193
00:08:14,619 --> 00:08:17,872
Boy, that story gets better
every time you hear it.

194
00:08:19,166 --> 00:08:20,500
Actually, I thought
the first two renditions

195
00:08:20,541 --> 00:08:22,169
were far more compelling.

196
00:08:22,210 --> 00:08:24,463
Previously, I felt sympathy
for the Leonard character,

197
00:08:24,503 --> 00:08:28,091
now I just find him
to be whiny and annoying.

198
00:08:28,133 --> 00:08:31,303
Eat your tangerine chicken.

199
00:08:31,345 --> 00:08:34,348
I'd love to, but I don't
have tangerine chicken.

200
00:08:36,183 --> 00:08:39,769
Thank you so much
for your stupid advice!

201
00:08:44,774 --> 00:08:46,776
Incredible.

202
00:08:46,818 --> 00:08:50,697
You managed to screw
up the screw-up.

203
00:08:52,157 --> 00:08:55,369
(knocking)

204
00:08:55,410 --> 00:08:57,620
I'm back.

205
00:08:57,662 --> 00:09:00,165
I'm sorry I yelled at you.

206
00:09:00,207 --> 00:09:01,624
It's not your fault.

207
00:09:01,666 --> 00:09:03,918
What happened?

208
00:09:03,960 --> 00:09:06,713
Well, I went over to Mike's
to make up with him.

209
00:09:06,754 --> 00:09:09,341
Yeah. No, I know that part.

210
00:09:09,383 --> 00:09:11,510
But he had already moved on.

211
00:09:11,550 --> 00:09:13,428
Already? That was quick.

212
00:09:13,470 --> 00:09:14,929
That's what I said to the woman

213
00:09:14,971 --> 00:09:18,725
who had her legs wrapped
around his neck!

214
00:09:19,934 --> 00:09:22,687
Oh, Penny,
I am so sorry.

215
00:09:22,729 --> 00:09:24,647
How could he do that?!

216
00:09:24,689 --> 00:09:28,776
Well, you know, you did
throw an 80-gig iPod...

217
00:09:28,818 --> 00:09:30,611
Yeah, no, how
could he do that?

218
00:09:30,653 --> 00:09:35,242
I swear to God I am done
with guys like that.

219
00:09:35,283 --> 00:09:38,661
You know, macho with the perfect
body and the hair and the money.

220
00:09:38,703 --> 00:09:41,039
Yeah, that must get old quick.

221
00:09:41,081 --> 00:09:42,832
You know, just once

222
00:09:42,874 --> 00:09:44,959
I would like
to go out with someone

223
00:09:45,001 --> 00:09:49,672
who is nice and honest
and who actually cares about me.

224
00:09:49,714 --> 00:09:51,550
(sobs)

225
00:09:51,591 --> 00:09:52,675
What about me?

226
00:09:52,717 --> 00:09:56,263
What about you what?

227
00:09:56,304 --> 00:10:00,600
What about if you
went out with me?

228
00:10:02,394 --> 00:10:04,396
Are you asking me out?

229
00:10:04,438 --> 00:10:07,482
Um, yes, I am...

230
00:10:07,524 --> 00:10:09,484
asking you out.

231
00:10:09,526 --> 00:10:11,736
Wow.

232
00:10:13,405 --> 00:10:15,865
I was just going off
your comment about
the nice guy...

233
00:10:15,907 --> 00:10:17,700
No, I know, I got that.
You know, and honest.

234
00:10:17,742 --> 00:10:18,701
Yeah, totally.

235
00:10:18,743 --> 00:10:19,786
So, but...
it's no big deal.

236
00:10:19,827 --> 00:10:20,787
Yes.

237
00:10:20,828 --> 00:10:23,748
Yes what?

238
00:10:23,790 --> 00:10:26,751
Yes, I will go out with you.

239
00:10:29,087 --> 00:10:31,672
Really?

240
00:10:31,714 --> 00:10:33,632
Yeah.

241
00:10:33,674 --> 00:10:35,427
Why not?

242
00:10:35,469 --> 00:10:37,804
I mean, what do I
have to lose?

243
00:10:37,845 --> 00:10:40,807
Yeah.

244
00:10:40,848 --> 00:10:43,310
That's the spirit.

245
00:10:46,605 --> 00:10:48,898
Show me your citrus peels.

246
00:10:48,940 --> 00:10:52,235
Gay wo kan ni de jud zi pee.

247
00:10:54,279 --> 00:10:55,614
Show me your citrus peels.

248
00:10:55,655 --> 00:10:58,158
Gay wo kan ni
de jud zi pee.

249
00:10:58,199 --> 00:11:00,827
Show me your...
Sheldon?

250
00:11:00,868 --> 00:11:02,621
Ai yah!

251
00:11:02,661 --> 00:11:04,956
I'm sorry.

252
00:11:04,998 --> 00:11:07,626
Look, do you have a second?

253
00:11:07,666 --> 00:11:10,545
A second what,
pair of underwear?

254
00:11:10,587 --> 00:11:12,797
I was just wondering if
I could talk to you.

255
00:11:12,839 --> 00:11:14,257
It's about Leonard.

256
00:11:14,299 --> 00:11:15,634
Why me?

257
00:11:15,674 --> 00:11:17,676
Why not Koothrappali
or Wolowitz?

258
00:11:17,718 --> 00:11:20,597
Well, Raj can't talk
to me unless he's drunk,

259
00:11:20,639 --> 00:11:23,350
and Wolowitz is, you
know, disgusting.

260
00:11:23,391 --> 00:11:26,102
Yes, I suppose he is.

261
00:11:27,728 --> 00:11:30,398
All I'm saying is,
you know Leonard the best.

262
00:11:30,440 --> 00:11:32,233
Not necessarily.

263
00:11:32,275 --> 00:11:33,652
I'm often surprised

264
00:11:33,692 --> 00:11:35,694
by my lack of familiarity
with Leonard.

265
00:11:35,736 --> 00:11:37,197
Just the other day
I discovered

266
00:11:37,238 --> 00:11:38,739
he not only has
a loofah,

267
00:11:38,781 --> 00:11:40,659
he hides it.

268
00:11:40,699 --> 00:11:43,286
Why do you suppose a man
would be ashamed of
having a loofah?

269
00:11:43,328 --> 00:11:45,872
I, myself, prefer to have
my excess epithelial cells

270
00:11:45,913 --> 00:11:47,415
slough off naturally,
but I don't condemn

271
00:11:47,457 --> 00:11:50,751
those who seek
to accelerate the process.

272
00:11:50,793 --> 00:11:53,630
...and until recently
I had no idea

273
00:11:53,672 --> 00:11:55,507
that despite his
lactose intolerance,

274
00:11:55,549 --> 00:11:58,051
he can tolerate small
amounts of non-fat ice cream

275
00:11:58,092 --> 00:11:59,969
without producing a noxious
gas that I maintain,

276
00:12:00,011 --> 00:12:02,889
in the right concentration,
could be weaponized.

277
00:12:02,930 --> 00:12:05,975
Leonard might come home.

278
00:12:06,017 --> 00:12:07,018
Can we talk in my apartment?

279
00:12:07,060 --> 00:12:10,188
We're not done?

280
00:12:10,230 --> 00:12:11,565
No.

281
00:12:11,606 --> 00:12:12,982
Eh, why not?

282
00:12:13,024 --> 00:12:14,901
We're already through
the looking glass anyway.

283
00:12:19,573 --> 00:12:22,200
Okay, so here's the thing...

284
00:12:22,242 --> 00:12:25,203
I guess you're aware
that Leonard asked me out.

285
00:12:25,245 --> 00:12:27,121
Well, he didn't actually
say anything,

286
00:12:27,163 --> 00:12:28,956
but when he came back
to the apartment

287
00:12:28,998 --> 00:12:30,917
he was doing a dance
that brought to mind

288
00:12:30,958 --> 00:12:33,336
the happy hippos in Fantasia.

289
00:12:33,378 --> 00:12:35,796
Oh, that's nice.

290
00:12:35,838 --> 00:12:37,716
Anyhow, the thing I wanted
to talk to you about

291
00:12:37,756 --> 00:12:39,926
is, you know, since Leonard
and I have become friends...

292
00:12:39,967 --> 00:12:41,052
I was just...

293
00:12:41,094 --> 00:12:43,012
You want to sit down?

294
00:12:43,054 --> 00:12:46,015
Oh, I wish it were that simple.

295
00:12:47,684 --> 00:12:49,185
See, I don't spend
much time here,

296
00:12:49,227 --> 00:12:51,521
and so I've never really chosen
a place to sit.

297
00:12:51,563 --> 00:12:53,732
Well, choose.

298
00:12:53,772 --> 00:12:56,693
There are a number
of options, and...

299
00:12:56,735 --> 00:12:59,237
I'm really not familiar enough
with the cushion densities,

300
00:12:59,279 --> 00:13:01,615
air flow patterns
and dispersion of sunlight

301
00:13:01,656 --> 00:13:03,908
to make an informed choice.

302
00:13:03,950 --> 00:13:05,702
All right, why don't you
just pick one at random

303
00:13:05,744 --> 00:13:07,828
and if you don't like it you can
sit somewhere else next time.

304
00:13:07,870 --> 00:13:10,039
No, no. That's crazy.

305
00:13:11,374 --> 00:13:14,835
You go ahead and talk
while I figure it out.

306
00:13:14,877 --> 00:13:16,713
Okay.

307
00:13:16,755 --> 00:13:19,132
Um, here's the thing.

308
00:13:19,173 --> 00:13:21,092
So I've known for
a while now

309
00:13:21,134 --> 00:13:22,260
that Leonard has had

310
00:13:22,302 --> 00:13:23,720
a little crush on me...

311
00:13:23,762 --> 00:13:25,846
A little crush?

312
00:13:25,888 --> 00:13:28,767
Well, I suppose so--
in the same way Menelaus

313
00:13:28,807 --> 00:13:31,352
had a "little crush"
on Helen of Troy.

314
00:13:31,394 --> 00:13:34,230
All right, yeah, I don't
really know who they are, but...

315
00:13:34,272 --> 00:13:35,856
Well, Menelaus was the
brother of Agamemnon...

316
00:13:35,898 --> 00:13:37,150
I don't care. I don't care.
Listen.

317
00:13:37,191 --> 00:13:39,694
The point is,

318
00:13:39,736 --> 00:13:42,572
Leonard isn't the kind of guy
I usually go out with.

319
00:13:42,614 --> 00:13:46,326
Leonard isn't the kind of guy
anyone usually goes out with.

320
00:13:46,367 --> 00:13:49,828
Would you be open
to rotating the couch
clockwise 30 degrees?

321
00:13:49,870 --> 00:13:51,665
No. What I'm saying is

322
00:13:51,706 --> 00:13:54,709
Leonard might be
different in a good way.

323
00:13:54,751 --> 00:13:58,129
Obviously, my usual choices
have not worked out so well.

324
00:13:58,171 --> 00:14:00,423
Your last one worked
out well for Koothrappali.

325
00:14:00,465 --> 00:14:02,425
He got a free iPod.

326
00:14:04,135 --> 00:14:06,095
Oh, glare.

327
00:14:06,137 --> 00:14:09,182
On the other hand, if things
don't go well with Leonard,

328
00:14:09,223 --> 00:14:11,351
I risk losing a
really good friend.

329
00:14:11,392 --> 00:14:13,394
I mean, I'm guessing he's not
looking for a fling.

330
00:14:13,436 --> 00:14:15,271
He's the kind of guy that
gets into a relationship

331
00:14:15,313 --> 00:14:17,440
for, I don't know,
like you would say,
light years.

332
00:14:17,482 --> 00:14:18,775
I would not say that.

333
00:14:18,817 --> 00:14:19,776
No one

334
00:14:19,818 --> 00:14:21,027
would say that.

335
00:14:21,068 --> 00:14:23,530
A light year is a unit
of distance not time.

336
00:14:23,571 --> 00:14:25,824
Thank you for the clarification.

337
00:14:32,038 --> 00:14:34,915
Draft.

338
00:14:34,957 --> 00:14:36,710
See, people hear
the word "year"

339
00:14:36,751 --> 00:14:38,503
and they think duration.

340
00:14:38,545 --> 00:14:40,213
"Foot-pound" has the
same problem--

341
00:14:40,254 --> 00:14:42,215
that's a unit of work,
not of weight.

342
00:14:42,256 --> 00:14:43,216
Right. Thanks.

343
00:14:43,257 --> 00:14:44,258
It's a common mistake.

344
00:14:44,300 --> 00:14:46,761
Not the first one
I've made today.

345
00:14:46,803 --> 00:14:48,722
Okay.

346
00:14:48,763 --> 00:14:52,684
I think this will be my seat.

347
00:14:52,726 --> 00:14:53,852
Sheldon, do you have
anything to say

348
00:14:53,892 --> 00:14:55,562
that has anything to do with,
you know,

349
00:14:55,603 --> 00:14:56,979
what I'm talking about?

350
00:14:57,021 --> 00:14:59,858
Well, let's see...

351
00:14:59,899 --> 00:15:02,109
We might consider
Schrodinger's cat.

352
00:15:02,151 --> 00:15:03,862
Schrodinger?

353
00:15:03,902 --> 00:15:05,405
Is that the woman in 2A?

354
00:15:05,446 --> 00:15:06,906
No, that's Mrs. Grossinger.

355
00:15:06,947 --> 00:15:09,033
And she doesn't have a cat,
she has a Mexican hairless.

356
00:15:09,075 --> 00:15:10,493
Annoying little animal.
Yip, yip, yip, yip.

357
00:15:10,535 --> 00:15:11,994
Sheldon.

358
00:15:12,036 --> 00:15:14,664
Sorry, you diverted me.

359
00:15:14,706 --> 00:15:18,585
Anyway, in 1935,
Erwin Schrodinger,

360
00:15:18,626 --> 00:15:20,670
in an attempt to explain
the Copenhagen interpretation

361
00:15:20,712 --> 00:15:21,796
of quantum physics,

362
00:15:21,838 --> 00:15:23,297
he proposed
an experiment

363
00:15:23,339 --> 00:15:26,843
where a cat is placed in a
box with a sealed vial of poison

364
00:15:26,885 --> 00:15:28,845
that will break open
at a random time.

365
00:15:28,887 --> 00:15:30,680
Now, since no one
knows when

366
00:15:30,722 --> 00:15:33,057
or if the
poison has been released,

367
00:15:33,099 --> 00:15:34,768
until the box is opened,

368
00:15:34,809 --> 00:15:38,646
the cat can be thought of
as both alive and dead.

369
00:15:42,609 --> 00:15:45,152
I'm sorry.
I don't get the point.

370
00:15:45,194 --> 00:15:47,321
Well, of course you don't get
it; I haven't made it yet.

371
00:15:49,949 --> 00:15:51,075
You'd have to be psychic
to get it,

372
00:15:51,117 --> 00:15:52,159
and there's no such
thing as "psychic."

373
00:15:52,201 --> 00:15:53,327
Sheldon, what's the point?!

374
00:15:53,369 --> 00:15:56,122
Just like Schrodinger's cat,

375
00:15:56,163 --> 00:15:58,499
your potential relationship
with Leonard right now

376
00:15:58,541 --> 00:16:00,668
can be thought of
as both good and bad.

377
00:16:00,710 --> 00:16:02,128
It is only by
opening the box

378
00:16:02,169 --> 00:16:04,798
that you'll find out
which it is.

379
00:16:04,839 --> 00:16:08,468
Okay, so you're saying I should
go out with Leonard.

380
00:16:08,509 --> 00:16:11,596
No, no, no, no, no.

381
00:16:11,638 --> 00:16:12,847
Let me start again.

382
00:16:12,889 --> 00:16:15,141
In 1935,
Erwin Schrodinger...

383
00:16:16,601 --> 00:16:19,395
Two seats right there.

384
00:16:21,188 --> 00:16:24,024
Chung shu shway?

385
00:16:25,735 --> 00:16:27,027
Shay shay.

386
00:16:31,532 --> 00:16:33,033
Sheldon, I think I've
made a mistake.

387
00:16:33,075 --> 00:16:34,452
I can see that.

388
00:16:34,494 --> 00:16:35,995
Unless you're planning on
running a marathon,

389
00:16:36,036 --> 00:16:37,914
choosing both stuffing
and mashed potatoes

390
00:16:37,956 --> 00:16:39,833
is a starch-filled redundancy.

391
00:16:39,874 --> 00:16:42,001
No, it's about Penny.

392
00:16:42,042 --> 00:16:44,170
A mistake involving Penny.

393
00:16:44,211 --> 00:16:46,965
Okay, you'll have
to narrow it down.

394
00:16:47,006 --> 00:16:50,217
I don't think I can go
out with her tonight.

395
00:16:50,259 --> 00:16:51,845
Then don't.

396
00:16:51,886 --> 00:16:54,305
Other people would
say "Why not?"

397
00:16:54,347 --> 00:16:56,891
Other people might
be interested.

398
00:16:56,933 --> 00:16:59,101
I'm going to talk anyway.

399
00:16:59,143 --> 00:17:01,521
I assumed you would.

400
00:17:01,562 --> 00:17:04,106
Now that I'm actually
about to go out with Penny,

401
00:17:04,148 --> 00:17:06,734
I'm not excited-- I'm nauseous.

402
00:17:06,776 --> 00:17:08,987
Ah, then your meal choice
is appropriate.

403
00:17:09,028 --> 00:17:11,280
Starch absorbs fluid which
reduces the amount of vomit

404
00:17:11,322 --> 00:17:13,741
available for violent expulsion.

405
00:17:13,783 --> 00:17:15,618
Right.
You also made

406
00:17:15,660 --> 00:17:17,119
a common grammatical mistake.

407
00:17:17,161 --> 00:17:19,288
You said "nauseous,"
when you meant "nauseated."

408
00:17:19,956 --> 00:17:21,875
But go on.

409
00:17:21,916 --> 00:17:25,043
Sheldon, this date is probably
my one chance with Penny.

410
00:17:25,085 --> 00:17:26,838
What happens if I blow it?

411
00:17:26,880 --> 00:17:28,631
Well, if we accept your premise

412
00:17:28,673 --> 00:17:31,968
and also accept the highly
improbable assumption

413
00:17:32,010 --> 00:17:35,013
that Penny is the only woman
in the world for you,

414
00:17:35,053 --> 00:17:36,597
then we can logically
conclude that the result

415
00:17:36,639 --> 00:17:38,224
of blowing it would be that
you end up

416
00:17:38,265 --> 00:17:41,101
a lonely, bitter old man
with no progeny.

417
00:17:41,143 --> 00:17:43,145
The image of any number
of evil lighthouse keepers

418
00:17:43,187 --> 00:17:45,899
from Scooby-Doo cartoons
comes to mind.

419
00:17:45,940 --> 00:17:48,735
You're not helping.

420
00:17:48,776 --> 00:17:50,319
All right, what response
on my part

421
00:17:50,361 --> 00:17:52,613
would bring this conversation
to a speedy conclusion?

422
00:17:52,655 --> 00:17:54,949
Tell me whether or not
to go through with the date.

423
00:17:54,991 --> 00:17:57,035
Schrodinger's cat.

424
00:17:57,076 --> 00:18:00,287
Wow, that's brilliant.

425
00:18:00,329 --> 00:18:03,041
You sound surprised.

426
00:18:04,959 --> 00:18:06,335
Mm...

427
00:18:06,377 --> 00:18:09,672
Ho-tsu shway zai li-toe.

428
00:18:23,477 --> 00:18:25,145
Hi.

429
00:18:25,187 --> 00:18:26,480
Hi.

430
00:18:26,522 --> 00:18:27,481
Come on in.

431
00:18:27,523 --> 00:18:30,735
Thank you.

432
00:18:30,777 --> 00:18:32,110
You look very nice.

433
00:18:32,152 --> 00:18:33,654
Thank you.
So do you.

434
00:18:33,696 --> 00:18:35,031
(both chuckle)

435
00:18:37,408 --> 00:18:40,078
I made an 8:00
reservation.

436
00:18:40,119 --> 00:18:41,245
Okay, yeah, great.

437
00:18:41,287 --> 00:18:42,789
Listen, um...

438
00:18:42,830 --> 00:18:44,498
maybe we should talk first.

439
00:18:44,540 --> 00:18:46,626
Oh.

440
00:18:46,667 --> 00:18:48,002
Okay.

441
00:18:49,169 --> 00:18:50,713
But before you
say anything,

442
00:18:50,755 --> 00:18:53,841
have you ever heard of
Schrodinger's cat?

443
00:18:53,883 --> 00:18:59,346
Actually, I've heard far too
much about Schrodinger's cat.

444
00:18:59,388 --> 00:19:00,807
Good.

445
00:19:06,395 --> 00:19:08,188
All right, the cat's alive.

446
00:19:08,230 --> 00:19:09,440
Let's go to dinner.

447
00:19:14,570 --> 00:19:16,572
Bee ti tsai nar! Bee ti!

448
00:19:16,614 --> 00:19:18,825
Xing gan jing bi ti
hou quai zoui.

449
00:19:18,866 --> 00:19:22,369
Zhe bu shi lui
ding jiao ta che!

450
00:19:22,411 --> 00:19:24,246
Crazy man.
Call the police!

451
00:19:24,288 --> 00:19:25,999
Bu bie da gei
tu shu guan.

452
00:19:26,040 --> 00:19:27,458
Bee ti tsai nar!

453
00:19:29,002 --> 00:19:30,920
Man niu zai wo chuang shang!

454
00:19:30,962 --> 00:19:33,089
Hen duo hen duo man niu!

455
00:19:33,131 --> 00:19:34,841
(scoffs)

456
00:19:34,882 --> 00:19:36,884
Ai yah!

